        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"The SGLT2 inhibitor cardio-renal evidence is one of the most important pharmacology paradigm shifts of the last decade and is heavily examined. Key trials: EMPA-REG OUTCOME (empagliflozin — 38% CV death reduction in T2DM + CVD); DAPA-HF (dapagliflozin — reduced HF events in HFrEF INCLUDING NON-DIABETIC patients); DAPA-CKD (dapagliflozin — reduced CKD progression regardless of diabetes). The exam point: SGLT2 inhibitors are now recommended for their CARDIO-RENAL BENEFITS even in patients without significant hyperglycaemia — the glucose-lowering effect is almost secondary to these organ-protective benefits."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"Glibenclamide in elderly or CKD patients is one of the most dangerous prescribing decisions in diabetes management. Glibenclamide has active metabolites that accumulate in renal impairment, causing PROLONGED severe hypoglycaemia that can last 24-36 hours and require prolonged IV dextrose infusion. In Ghana, glibenclamide remains widely available and commonly prescribed. At every diabetes review involving an elderly patient or patient with CKD on glibenclamide, actively switch to gliclazide MR — this single prescribing change prevents hypoglycaemia admissions and deaths."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"Euglycaemic DKA with SGLT2 inhibitors is a diagnosis that is missed because the glucose is NEAR-NORMAL — the diagnostic criterion of 'glucose >11 mmol/L' is absent, yet ketoacidosis is present. Any patient on an SGLT2 inhibitor presenting with nausea, vomiting, malaise, and abdominal pain — even with near-normal glucose — should have BLOOD KETONES checked. The sick day rules for SGLT2 inhibitors: HOLD the drug when acutely unwell with vomiting, diarrhoea, or reduced oral intake, and for at least 3 days before elective surgery. Document this counselling at every prescription."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"In DKA management, hourly monitoring of blood KETONES (not just glucose) is the primary marker of treatment response. The resolution criteria are: blood ketones <0.6 mmol/L AND pH >7.3 AND blood glucose <14 mmol/L. Glucose may normalise before ketones clear — stopping insulin infusion when glucose reaches 'normal' but ketones remain elevated is a recognised error. The fixed-rate insulin infusion continues until ALL three criteria are met; a glucose-containing fluid is added to the IV regimen when glucose falls below 14 mmol/L to prevent hypoglycaemia while the insulin infusion continues to clear ketones."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"HbA1c has important limitations that affect result interpretation in common Ghanaian clinical scenarios: (1) HAEMOGLOBIN VARIANTS (HbS, HbC, HbF) — common in Ghana; can falsely LOWER or RAISE HbA1c depending on the assay method; HPLC (high-performance liquid chromatography) can identify variant haemoglobins and use chromatography to separate them, improving accuracy; (2) HAEMOLYTIC ANAEMIA (including sickle cell crises, G6PD deficiency episodes) — shortened RBC lifespan → falsely LOWER HbA1c; (3) IRON DEFICIENCY ANAEMIA — falsely RAISES HbA1c; (4) When HbA1c is unreliable, FRUCTOSAMINE (reflects 2-3 week average glucose) is the alternative. Flag any HbA1c result where these conditions apply."},
        ]
